    "thm-emg-maxwell-gauss-propagation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-emg-fieldstrength-positive-symmetric-reduction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The six evolution equations for $(e,b)$ have principal part\n$$n(e)-\\operatorname{curl}_h b=\\text{terms with no derivatives of }(e,b),\\qquad n(b)+\\operatorname{curl}_h e=\\text{terms with no derivatives of }(e,b).$$\nFor a spatial covector $\\xi$, let $C(\\xi)v:=\\xi^\\sharp\\times v$ in the oriented orthonormal frame.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-emg-lorentz-hodge-calculus",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N,o)$ have the oriented, time-oriented Lorentzian structure and volume form of [[def-emg-lorentzian-forms]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-gr-metric-volume-form-and-current-flux",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "This item\nfixes the metric volume form, the flux of a current through oriented $3$-faces\nand finite-chain charges.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "def-emg-electrovacuum-cauchy-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Indices are raised with $g^{-1}$; $\\star_g$ is the Lorentzian Hodge star of [[lem-emg-lorentz-hodge-calculus]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $F:D\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be continuous and locally Lipschitz in the state variable, and let $(t_0,x_0)\\in D$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-c1-dependence-of-solutions-on-initial-data",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Fix data $(t_0,x_0)\\in D$ and a compact time\ninterval $I=[t_0-h,t_0+h]$ on which the corresponding solutions through nearby\ninitial states all exist.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be\n$C^1$, and let $a\\in U$.  If $Df(a)$ is invertible, then there are open sets\n$V,W\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ with $a\\in V\\subseteq U$ and $f(a)\\in W$ such that\n$f|_V:V\\to W$ is bijective.  Its inverse $g:W\\to V$ is $C^1$, and",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Put $U:=dF$ and $V:=d\\star_gF$. They are continuous three-forms. By [F1], $\\iota_nU=\\iota_nV=0$ throughout the evolution. In a local frame with timelike vector $n$, a three-form annihilated by $n$ has only its spatial volume component, so there are unique continuous scalars $q,r$ with $U=q\\,\\iota_n\\mathrm{vol}_g$ and $V=r\\,\\iota_n\\mathrm{vol}_g$. On $\\Sigma_0$, the spatial components of $dF$ and $d\\star_gF$ are respectively $(D_i b^i)\\mathrm{vol}_h$ and, up to the fixed orientation sign, $(D_i e^i)\\mathrm{vol}_h$. Thus $q=r=0$ on the initial slice.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "L1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Since $dU=d^2F=0$ and $dV=d^2\\star_gF=0$ distributionally, the coordinate formula for exterior derivative gives $\\partial_\\mu(\\sqrt{|\\det g|}\\,q n^\\mu)=0$ and the same equation for $r$. The identity $\\partial_\\mu(\\sqrt{|\\det g|}n^\\mu)=\\sqrt{|\\det g|}\\,\\nabla_\\mu n^\\mu$ follows from $\\Gamma^\\mu{}_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\nu\\log\\sqrt{|\\det g|}$, itself obtained by differentiating the determinant and using the Levi-Civita formula. Therefore $n(q)+q\\,\\operatorname{div}_g n=0$ and $n(r)+r\\,\\operatorname{div}_g n=0$ in the distributional sense.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "The field $n$ is $C^1$ and transverse to $\\Sigma_0$ by hypothesis. Its local flow map $\\Phi(\\tau,x)$ is $C^1$ and the differential at $\\tau=0$ maps the time direction to $n$ and the slice directions identically, hence is invertible. By [F3], after shrinking, $(\\tau,x)$ are flow coordinates. Pullback preserves distributional closedness of these continuous three-forms under the $C^1$ flow chart: test against compactly supported smooth functions, use the change-of-variables formula, and approximate the resulting compactly supported $C^1$ test functions in their $C^1$ norm. In these coordinates $n=\\partial_\\tau$ and $\\mathrm{vol}_g=J(\\tau,x)d\\tau\\wedge dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3$ with continuous $J>0$. Thus $dU=0$ reads $\\partial_\\tau(Jq)=0$ distributionally, and $dV=0$ reads $\\partial_\\tau(Jr)=0$. A continuous function whose distributional derivative in $\\tau$ vanishes is constant in $\\tau$: convolving in $\\tau$ gives smooth functions with zero derivative, and uniform convergence on compact subcylinders passes that constancy to the original function; testing against spatial test functions first gives the same conclusion when both variables are present. Their initial values vanish by step 1.1, so $q=r=0$ throughout the flow neighborhood.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "The identities $U=q\\,\\iota_n\\mathrm{vol}_g$ and $V=r\\,\\iota_n\\mathrm{vol}_g$ now give $dF=0$ and $d\\star_gF=0$. The argument uses only the Maxwell evolution, its initial Gauss constraints, and $d^2=0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.1",
            "L1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
