    "lem-emg-coupled-reduced-einstein-evolution": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "def-emg-electrovacuum-cauchy-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix $c>0$, $G_N>0$, $\\mu_0>0$, and $\\Lambda\\in\\mathbb R$, and put",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "def-emg-periodic-high-sobolev-state",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}$, its normalized measure, the componentwise real Sobolev spaces $H^s(\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi};\\mathbb R^m)$, and their weak-derivative and Fourier-coefficient conventions be exactly those of [[def-gr-periodic-torus-and-sobolev-spaces]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L3",
          "source": "thm-emg-symmetric-hyperbolic-research-supplier",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $s\\ge5$ be an integer, let $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, and let $A^\\mu,B$ ($\\mu=0,1,2,3$) be a periodic symmetric-hyperbolic coefficient family on $I\\times\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}\\times O$ in the exact sense of [[def-gr-periodic-symmetric-hyperbolic-system]]: the coefficient maps are smooth, each $A^\\mu$ is a real symmetric matrix, and $A^0$ is positive definite.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-emg-fieldstrength-positive-symmetric-reduction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In the adapted coordinates its time matrix is $\\alpha^{-1}I_6$, and every coordinate spatial matrix is symmetric.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L4",
          "source": "lem-gr-reduced-einstein-principal-part",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then the reduced Einstein equation can be solved for the second time derivatives in the form\n$$\\bar\\nabla_0p_{ab}=-(g^{00})^{-1}\\Bigl[2g^{0i}\\bar\\nabla_ip_{ab}+g^{ij}\\bar\\nabla_iq_{abj}+R_{ab}(x,u,T;\\kappa,\\Lambda,\\bar g)\\Bigr],$$\nwhere the source is smooth in the first-order metric state and the prescribed matter data, with no derivatives of $T$, and includes $2\\kappa(T_{ab}-\\tfrac12(g^{cd}T_{cd})g_{ab})+2\\Lambda g_{ab}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L5",
          "source": "lem-emg-fixed-smooth-background-harmonic-data",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Fix a smooth positive reference metric $\\gamma$ on $\\Sigma$ and use the product background $\\bar g=-(dx^0)^2+\\gamma_{ij}(x)dx^i dx^j$ near $\\{x^0=0\\}\\times\\Sigma$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "In the admissible open state set, $g^{00}<0$ and $(g^{ij})>0$ make the metric time matrix in [L4] positive definite. On a compact $K'$, its least eigenvalue is bounded below by a positive constant. The lapse condition gives the Maxwell time matrix $\\alpha^{-1}I_6>0$, also uniformly on $K'$. Therefore the combined time matrix is the block sum of these two positive matrices.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L4",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "For a spatial covector $\\xi$, the metric wave spatial matrices are symmetric by [L4], while the Maxwell spatial matrices are symmetric by [F1]; the shift contributes a scalar identity block and preserves symmetry. The Einstein stress $Q(g,F)$ is algebraic in $(g,g^{-1},F)$, so its components contain no derivatives of $U$. The Maxwell tetrad and Hodge coefficients depend smoothly on $(g,g^{-1})$, and their derivatives multiply only the state components; because $p,q$ are included in $U$, these are smooth zero-order terms. There are no off-diagonal derivative blocks between the metric and Maxwell equations.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L1",
            "F1",
            "L4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "The conditions $g^{00}<0$, $(g^{ij})>0$, $\\alpha>0$, and $h>0$ define an open state domain; by shrinking around the orthogonal initial state from [L5], choose compact $K\\Subset\\operatorname{int}K'$ on which all coefficient derivatives and the positive time-matrix lower bounds are uniform. Thus the complete reduced coefficient family satisfies the smoothness, symmetry, positivity, compact-state, and $H^s$ bounds required by [L2]–[L3]. Applying the draft solver conditionally gives the claimed local reduced solution for arbitrary admissible $U_0$; no constraint or physical-equation recovery has been used.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "given",
            "L2",
            "L3",
            "L5",
            "construct"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement quantifies over initial states with values in K on the nonempty torus. If K is empty, no such U0 exists and its per-datum existence conclusion has no instance; no empty-range solution is asserted."
        },
        {
