    "thm-emg-einstein-gauge-propagation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-emg-maxwell-gauss-propagation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a smooth oriented four-manifold with a time-oriented $C^1$ Lorentzian metric $g$ of signature $(-,+,+,+)$. Let a neighborhood of the initial slice $\\Sigma_0$ have a $C^1$ spacelike foliation with a $C^1$ future unit normal field $n$, transverse to $\\Sigma_0$. Here the normal's $C^1$ regularity is an explicit hypothesis; a merely $C^1$ defining time function alone need not provide it. Smooth coordinate time levels with a $C^1$ metric satisfy this hypothesis. Let $F$ be a $C^1$ real two-form on this neighborhood. Define its electric and magnetic spatial vectors by $e_i=F(\\mathsf e_i,n)$ and $b_i=\\tfrac12\\epsilon_i{}^{jk}F(\\mathsf e_j,\\mathsf e_k)$ in a positively oriented orthonormal spatial frame. Suppose the six evolution equations are\n$$\\iota_n dF=0,\\qquad \\iota_n d(\\star_gF)=0,$$\nand suppose the initial Gauss constraints are $D_ie^i=0$ and $D_ib^i=0$ on $\\Sigma_0$, where $D$ is the Levi-Civita derivative of its induced $C^1$ Riemannian metric $h$. These six equations are the normal-slot component equations of the field-strength reduction [[lem-emg-fieldstrength-positive-symmetric-reduction]]; their displayed first-derivative formulation is used here directly, without assuming that supplier's smooth-field hypotheses. Then $dF=0$ and $d(\\star_gF)=0$ on a neighborhood of each point of $\\Sigma_0$. No Einstein equation, stress conservation law, smooth potential, or global exactness of $F$ is needed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "lem-emg-maxwell-stress-conservation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In the source-free case $J=0$, $\\nabla^aT^{\\rm EM}_{ab}=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L3",
          "source": "thm-emg-gauss-codazzi-maxwell-constraints",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then the induced tuple $(\\Sigma,h,K,e,b)$ satisfies all four constraints in [[def-emg-constrained-electrovacuum-data]], namely the two Maxwell divergence constraints and",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-gr-einstein-residual-and-subsidiary-identity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(b)** if $(g,T)$ solves the reduced Einstein equation on an open set $U$ and\n$\\nabla^aT_{ab}=0$ on $U$, then the gauge vector satisfies the homogeneous\nlinear wave equation\n$$\\Box_gH_b+\\operatorname{Ric}_{ba}H^a=0\\qquad\\text{on }U,$$\nwhere $\\Box_gH_b=\\nabla^a\\nabla_aH_b$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-gr-reduced-einstein-principal-part",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The resulting first-order system for $u$ is symmetric hyperbolic on every\nregion where $g^{00}<0$ and the spatial block $(g^{ij})$ is positive definite:\nthe coefficient of $\\bar\\nabla_0u$ is the block diagonal\n$\\operatorname{diag}(I,-g^{00}I,g^{ij}I)$ (on the three slots\n$g,p,q$), which is positive definite there, and the coefficients of the spatial\nderivatives are symmetric.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "By [L1], the reduced Maxwell evolution and its initial divergence constraints give the full source-free Maxwell equations. The stress-conservation interface [L2] then yields $\\nabla^aT_{ab}=0$ before using the unreduced Einstein equations. Thus [F1] applies and the harmonic vector obeys its homogeneous linear wave equation.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L1",
            "L2",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Since $H=0$ as a field on $\\Sigma_0$, every tangential covariant derivative of $H$ vanishes there. The Einstein constraints [L3] say that the unreduced residual has zero normal-normal and normal-tangential components. In an initially orthogonal frame with $g_{00}=-1$ and $g_{0i}=0$, the residual formula of [F1] gives $E_{00}=\\tfrac12\\nabla_0H_0$ and $E_{0i}=\\tfrac12\\nabla_0H_i$: indeed $\\nabla_iH_j=\\nabla_iH_0=0$ on the slice, and $\\nabla_cH^c=-\\nabla_0H_0$. Hence $\\nabla_nH=0$ initially.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L3",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Fix a point of $\\Sigma_0$ and a relatively compact coordinate ball around it. Write the wave equation in [F1] as the first-order symmetric hyperbolic system for $W=(H,\\partial_sH,\\partial_iH)$ supplied by [F2]. On a sufficiently small compact cylinder its time matrix satisfies $A^0\\succeq aI$ for some $a>0$, all spatial matrices are bounded, and all lower-order coefficients are bounded. Choose $\\lambda> a^{-1}\\sup_{|\\nu|=1}\\|\\nu_iA^i\\|$. For $s\\ge0$ let $B_s$ be the Euclidean ball of radius $r-\\lambda s$ about the chosen point and set $\\mathcal E(s)=\\int_{B_s}W^{\\mathsf T}A^0W\\,dx$. The symmetric system's product rule and spatial integration by parts give $\\mathcal E'(s)\\le C\\mathcal E(s)-\\int_{\\partial B_s}W^{\\mathsf T}(\\lambda A^0+\\nu_iA^i)W\\,dS$. The boundary matrix is positive semidefinite because $\\lambda a>\\sup_{|\\nu|=1}\\|\\nu_iA^i\\|$; therefore $\\mathcal E'\\le C\\mathcal E$. The zero initial values established in steps 1.1 and 1.2, together with Gronwall, force $\\mathcal E=0$ in the shrinking cone. Reversing time proves the same on the past cone. Thus $H=0$ in a neighborhood of the chosen point, and the point was arbitrary.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "When $H=0$, the harmonic-reduced Einstein equation differs from the unreduced equation by terms linear in $\\nabla H$, which now vanish. Step 1.1 already gives both Maxwell equations, so the full coupled Einstein–Maxwell equations hold near the initial surface. The conclusion remains conditional on the CP01 draft statements [L2]–[L3], whose files are listed in batch 6 but remain precheck-pending.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "step 1.1",
            "L2",
            "L3",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement concludes near each point of the supplied initial hypersurface; proof step 2.1 fixes such a point and a ball. It does not select data from an empty indexed family."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Proof step 1.2 derives both zero H and zero normal derivative from the initial gauge and Einstein constraints; proof step 2.1 then gives E=0 from Eprime<=CE and zero initial energy, including H identically zero."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
