          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the draft local symmetric-hyperbolic supplier [[thm-emg-symmetric-hyperbolic-research-supplier]] with its stated integer-$s$, coefficient, compact-state, choice and norm hypotheses.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-emg-symmetric-hyperbolic-research-supplier",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "This theorem supplies only the analytic conclusion for a system whose coefficient hypotheses have already been verified.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L5",
          "source": "thm-emg-symmetric-hyperbolic-research-supplier",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $s\\ge5$ be an integer, let $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, and let $A^\\mu,B$ ($\\mu=0,1,2,3$) be a periodic symmetric-hyperbolic coefficient family on $I\\times\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}\\times O$ in the exact sense of [[def-gr-periodic-symmetric-hyperbolic-system]]: the coefficient maps are smooth, each $A^\\mu$ is a real symmetric matrix, and $A^0$ is positive definite.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-emg-maxwell-gauss-propagation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a smooth oriented four-manifold with a time-oriented $C^1$ Lorentzian metric $g$ of signature $(-,+,+,+)$. Let a neighborhood of the initial slice $\\Sigma_0$ have a $C^1$ spacelike foliation with a $C^1$ future unit normal field $n$, transverse to $\\Sigma_0$. Here the normal's $C^1$ regularity is an explicit hypothesis; a merely $C^1$ defining time function alone need not provide it. Smooth coordinate time levels with a $C^1$ metric satisfy this hypothesis. Let $F$ be a $C^1$ real two-form on this neighborhood. Define its electric and magnetic spatial vectors by $e_i=F(\\mathsf e_i,n)$ and $b_i=\\tfrac12\\epsilon_i{}^{jk}F(\\mathsf e_j,\\mathsf e_k)$ in a positively oriented orthonormal spatial frame. Suppose the six evolution equations are\n$$\\iota_n dF=0,\\qquad \\iota_n d(\\star_gF)=0,$$\nand suppose the initial Gauss constraints are $D_ie^i=0$ and $D_ib^i=0$ on $\\Sigma_0$, where $D$ is the Levi-Civita derivative of its induced $C^1$ Riemannian metric $h$. These six equations are the normal-slot component equations of the field-strength reduction [[lem-emg-fieldstrength-positive-symmetric-reduction]]; their displayed first-derivative formulation is used here directly, without assuming that supplier's smooth-field hypotheses. Then $dF=0$ and $d(\\star_gF)=0$ on a neighborhood of each point of $\\Sigma_0$. No Einstein equation, stress conservation law, smooth potential, or global exactness of $F$ is needed.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "5.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L4",
          "source": "lem-emg-maxwell-stress-conservation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In the source-free case $J=0$, $\\nabla^aT^{\\rm EM}_{ab}=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-emg-einstein-gauge-propagation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the source-free Maxwell divergence constraints and the four Einstein Hamiltonian/momentum constraints hold on $\\Sigma_0$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "5.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-gr-periodic-symmetric-hyperbolic-existence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice]]).",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-gr-periodic-symmetric-hyperbolic-smooth-persistence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice]]).",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-gr-periodic-symmetric-hyperbolic-uniqueness-stability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(b) Continuous dependence.** Let $K'\\subseteq O$ be compact with\n$K\\subseteq\\operatorname{int}K'$ and let $T>0$ be a time for which the existence\ntheorem [[thm-gr-periodic-symmetric-hyperbolic-existence]] produces solutions\nfrom every datum with values in $K$ and norm at most $R$, with values in $K'$\nand the bounds $C(1+R)$ of that theorem.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-gr-smooth-composition-and-commutator-estimates",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $s\\ge5$ be an integer, let $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, let $I$ be an open time interval, let $I_0\\subseteq I$ be a compact interval, and let $F:I\\times\\mathbb T^3\\times O\\to\\mathbb R^{m'}$ and $A:I\\times\\mathbb T^3\\times O\\to M_{m'}(\\mathbb R)$ be smooth.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-gr-periodic-sobolev-algebra",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(b)** the pointwise product $uv$ of the continuous representatives lies in\n$H^k$ and\n$$\\|uv\\|_{H^k}\\le C_k\\,\\|u\\|_{H^k}\\,\\|v\\|_{H^k};$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-gr-periodic-sobolev-embedding",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "The constraints in the Statement are the normal projections and Maxwell Gauss constraints of the model [L1] under the stated $K,e,b$ conventions. Initialization [L3] gives $H=0$ and the compatible derivative variables $p=\\bar\\nabla_0g$, $q_i=\\bar\\nabla_i g$ on the initial slice. The initial state lies in $H^s$: $h\\in H^{s+1}$ gives $q_i\\in H^s$, $K,e,b\\in H^s$, and the harmonic normal derivatives are in $H^s$ by the smooth composition estimates [F7] on the compact range of the uniformly positive $h$. Compactness of $\\mathbb T^3$ and $h\\ge c_0I$ place the initial state in a compact subset of the open admissible set.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L1",
            "L2",
            "L3",
            "F7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "By [F1], the metric and Maxwell principal blocks are symmetric with positive time matrices and have no derivative coupling. At the initial orthogonal state $g^{00}<0$, $(g^{ij})>0$ and $\\alpha>0$; these strict inequalities persist on a compact admissible state neighborhood, so the complete coefficient family meets the positivity and compact-state assumptions of [L5]. Applying that draft supplier conditionally gives the reduced solution and its $L^\\infty H^s\\cap C H^{s-1}$ and $\\partial_tU\\in L^\\infty H^{s-1}$ bounds. The actual inspected generic existence theorem [F4] states the same periodic coefficient interface and is marked published with precheck pending; it is independent current support, not acceptance of the CP01 draft supplier.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "L5",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "The first-order metric equations impose $\\bar\\nabla_0g=p$ and $\\bar\\nabla_0q_i-\\bar\\nabla_ip=\\mathcal C_i$, where $\\mathcal C_i=[\\bar\\nabla_0,\\bar\\nabla_i]g$ is the fixed background-curvature commutator. For $Z_i=q_i-\\bar\\nabla_i g$, subtract the commutator identity for $g$ from the equation for $q_i$. The derivative-slot connection terms cancel and give a homogeneous linear ODE along each coordinate time line for $Z_i$; its initial value is zero by [L3], so uniqueness for a linear ODE gives $Z_i=0$. Thus $q_i=\\bar\\nabla_i g$ throughout the interval.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "L3",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "The Fourier norm identity $\\|g\\|_{H^{s+1}}^2\\asymp\\|g\\|_{H^s}^2+\\sum_i\\|\\partial_i g\\|_{H^s}^2$, together with step 3.1 and the smooth background coefficients, gives $g\\in L^\\infty H^{s+1}$. The same identity at one lower order and the $C H^{s-1}$ continuity of the first-order state give $g\\in C H^s$. The tetrad reconstruction of $F$ is a smooth algebraic function of $(g,e,b)$ on the compact admissible state set; [F7] gives $F\\in L^\\infty H^s\\cap C H^{s-1}$. For $s\\ge5$, the reduced equation writes $U_t$ as a smooth expression in $(U,\\partial_iU)$; since $U\\in C H^{s-1}$, its right side is continuous in $H^{s-2}$. Thus $U_t\\in C H^{s-2}$, and differentiating this expression once gives $U_{tt}\\in C H^{s-3}$. At the base order $s=5$, these spaces are $H^3$ and $H^2$. The identities $p=\\bar\\nabla_0g$ and $q_i=\\bar\\nabla_i g$ then give the third spatial derivatives of $g$ from two spatial derivatives of $q$, the $t^2x$ derivatives from one spatial derivative of $p_t$, and $t^3$ derivatives from $p_{tt}$; the other derivatives of total order at most three follow directly from $p,q\\in C H^4$ and $p_t\\in C H^3$. Since $H^2(\\mathbb T^3)\\hookrightarrow C^0$, all these derivatives are continuous. The same smooth composition argument gives $F\\in C^2$ from $(g,e,b)$, so the classical regularity required by [F2] and [F3] holds.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "L2",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F7",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "Maxwell propagation [F2] now gives $dF=d\\star_gF=0$. The conditional stress identity [L4] gives $\\nabla^aT_{ab}=0$, so [F3] applies with the initial Einstein constraints and $H=0$ from [L3]; hence the reduced metric equation is the full Einstein equation. Smooth persistence [F5] applies to smooth data on the same interval. Finally, [F6] applies to any two reduced solutions on a common interval with these uniform compact-state and $H^s$ bounds and yields both uniqueness and the claimed $C H^{s-1}$ continuous-dependence estimate.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "L3",
            "L4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "step": "6.1",
          "claim": "(Local smooth data on an arbitrary slice.) Fix a point of a smooth initial three-manifold and choose an orthogonal spacetime chart over a relatively compact slice ball, with the prescribed smooth background connection. The initial harmonic state from [L3] and its admissibility inequalities take values, after shrinking the ball, in a convex finite-dimensional neighborhood whose closure lies in the open symmetric-hyperbolic state set. Extend the state by a smooth cutoff to a constant admissible state on a periodic three-torus; convexity keeps all values in that neighborhood. Extend the coefficient matrices by the same spatial cutoff, interpolating $A^0$ to the identity and each symmetric $A^i$ and the lower-order term to zero. The convex cone of positive matrices preserves a positive time matrix, and the spatial matrices remain symmetric. The conditional local solver in [F1] therefore gives a smooth reduced solution on a torus cylinder, which satisfies the original reduced equations wherever the cutoff is one. The derivative-definition relations remain valid along the time lines there, by the same homogeneous commutator ODE as step 3.1. The Maxwell divergence residuals vanish near the chosen point by the local flow argument of [F2]. The Einstein constraints and $H=0$ hold on the initial ball; the local cone-energy proof of [F3] therefore gives $H=0$ in a smaller domain of dependence. On that neighborhood the reduced solution solves the full source-free system. This proves the additional local existence clause without imposing constraints on the auxiliary extension outside the slice ball.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "L3",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement fixes the nonempty periodic torus; its additional smooth clause is near each initial point. Neither asserts a solution selected from an empty data domain."
