    "prop-emg-local-sobolev-stability-continuation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-emg-local-high-sobolev-electrovacuum",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then there are $T>0$ and a solution of the full source-free Einstein–Maxwell system on $(-T,T)\\times\\mathbb T^3$ with\n$$g\\in L^\\infty H^{s+1}\\cap C^0H^s,\\qquad \\partial_tg\\in L^\\infty H^s\\cap C^0H^{s-1},\\qquad F\\in L^\\infty H^s\\cap C^0H^{s-1}.$$\nThe initial two-form is reconstructed by $F(n,\\mathsf e_i)=-e_i$ and $F(\\mathsf e_i,\\mathsf e_j)=\\epsilon_{ijk}b_k$; no independent Maxwell normal derivative is prescribed.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-emg-symmetric-hyperbolic-research-supplier",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $s\\ge5$ be an integer, let $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, and let $A^\\mu,B$ ($\\mu=0,1,2,3$) be a periodic symmetric-hyperbolic coefficient family on $I\\times\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}\\times O$ in the exact sense of [[def-gr-periodic-symmetric-hyperbolic-system]]: the coefficient maps are smooth, each $A^\\mu$ is a real symmetric matrix, and $A^0$ is positive definite.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-gr-periodic-symmetric-hyperbolic-uniqueness-stability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(b) Continuous dependence.** Let $K'\\subseteq O$ be compact with\n$K\\subseteq\\operatorname{int}K'$ and let $T>0$ be a time for which the existence\ntheorem [[thm-gr-periodic-symmetric-hyperbolic-existence]] produces solutions\nfrom every datum with values in $K$ and norm at most $R$, with values in $K'$\nand the bounds $C(1+R)$ of that theorem.",
          "uses": [
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-gr-periodic-symmetric-hyperbolic-existence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "for every $u_0\\in H^s(\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi};\\mathbb R^m)$ taking values in $K$ with\n$\\|u_0\\|_{H^s}\\le R$ there is a Sobolev solution\n$u:(-T,T)\\to H^s(\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi};\\mathbb R^m)$ of $(\\star)$ with\n$u(0)=u_0$ which is classical and satisfies\n$$u\\in L^\\infty((-T,T);H^s)\\cap C^0((-T,T);H^{s-1}),\\quad \\partial_tu\\in L^\\infty((-T,T);H^{s-1}),$$\n$$\\sup_{|t|<T}\\|u(t)\\|_{H^s}\\le C(1+R),\\qquad \\sup_{|t|<T}\\|\\partial_tu(t)\\|_{H^{s-1}}\\le C(1+R),$$\nand whose values lie in $K'$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-emg-periodic-high-sobolev-state",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The high-regularity initial metric and electromagnetic data are $h\\in H^{s+1}$, $K,e,b\\in H^s$, with $h$ uniformly positive definite; the product state and its first spatial metric derivatives lie in $H^s$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-gr-smooth-composition-and-commutator-estimates",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $s\\ge5$ be an integer, let $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, let $I$ be an open time interval, let $I_0\\subseteq I$ be a compact interval, and let $F:I\\times\\mathbb T^3\\times O\\to\\mathbb R^{m'}$ and $A:I\\times\\mathbb T^3\\times O\\to M_{m'}(\\mathbb R)$ be smooth.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-gr-periodic-sobolev-algebra",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $k\\ge4$ be an integer and let\n$u,v\\in H^k(\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi};\\mathbb R)$ with the objects and notation of\n[[def-gr-periodic-torus-and-sobolev-spaces]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-gr-periodic-sobolev-embedding",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(a)** There is a unique representative $u^*\\in C^m(\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi};\\mathbb R^N)$\nof the class $u$, and for every $|\\alpha|\\le m$ the series\n$\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^3}(ik)^\\alpha\\widehat u(k)\\chi_k$ converges absolutely and\nuniformly on $\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}$ to $D^\\alpha u^*$; moreover there is\n$C=C(s,m,N)$ with\n$$\\|u^*\\|_{C^m}\\le C\\,\\|u\\|_{H^s}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "The reduced equation has the form $A^0(U)\\partial_tU+A^i(U)\\partial_iU+B(U)=0$ in the sense of [[def-gr-periodic-symmetric-hyperbolic-system]]. On the compact state set, $A^0(U)^{-1}$ and the coefficients are bounded smooth functions. Since $\\|U\\|_{H^s}$ is uniformly bounded and $H^{s-1}$ is an algebra for $s\\ge5$, the equation and the composition estimates [F4] give a uniform bound $\\|\\partial_tU\\|_{H^{s-1}}\\le C_R$; this agrees with the bound in [F3]. This supplies endpoint time-derivative control even if it was not separately included in the displayed regularity.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "For each finite Fourier projection $P_N$, every coefficient of $P_NU$ is absolutely continuous and its derivative is the corresponding coefficient of $P_N\\partial_tU$. Thus $\\|P_NU(t)-P_NU(t')\\|_{H^{s-1}}\\le C_R|t-t'|$. Letting $N\\to\\infty$ and using Fourier convergence in $H^{s-1}$ gives the same estimate for $U(t)-U(t')$. Hence $U(t)$ has a strong $H^{s-1}$ limit $U_T$ as $t\\uparrow T$.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "For each finite frequency set $\\{|k|\\le N\\}$, strong convergence of its coefficients gives\n$$\\sum_{|k|\\le N}(1+|k|^2)^s|\\widehat U_T(k)|^2=\\lim_{t\\uparrow T}\\sum_{|k|\\le N}(1+|k|^2)^s|\\widehat U(t,k)|^2\\le R^2,$$\nwhere $R=\\sup_{t<T}\\|U(t)\\|_{H^s}$. Taking the supremum over $N$ in the Fourier definition [F4] proves $U_T\\in H^s$ and $\\|U_T\\|_{H^s}\\le R$. By the embedding in [F4], $U(t)\\to U_T$ uniformly in space. Since each $U(t,x)$ lies in the fixed compact state set, the closedness of that set gives $U_T(x)$ in the same set for every $x$.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "The derivative identities from [F1] pass to $U_T$: $q_T=\\bar\\nabla_i g_T$ because these relations are continuous in the strong $H^{s-1}$ limit. Since $q_T\\in H^s$, the Fourier norm identity gives $g_T\\in H^{s+1}$; likewise $p_T,e_T,b_T\\in H^s$. The Maxwell and Einstein constraints are continuous under this convergence: they involve at most two spatial derivatives of $h_T$ and one derivative of $K_T,e_T,b_T$, which are controlled in $H^{s-2}$ by the converging first-order state and its derivative relations; for $s\\ge5$, Sobolev embedding and the algebra estimates make all products continuous. The harmonic vector is an algebraic smooth function of $(g_T,p_T,q_T)$, so $H_T=0$ as the limit of $H(t)=0$. Therefore $U_T$ determines constrained data of precisely the regularity required by [F1].",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.1",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "Restart the same harmonic-reduced coefficient system at time $T$ with the actual complete state $U_T$, keeping the fixed background and coordinates unchanged. The coefficients are smooth on the same time-state domain, $T$ is interior to its time interval, and step 3.1 supplies an $H^s$ datum with compact admissible range and bounded norm. Applying [L1] (equivalently [F3] after translating time) gives a reduced solution $V$ on $(T-\\varepsilon,T+\\varepsilon)$ with $V(T)=U_T$. This uses no reinitialization to lapse one, shift zero, or the special metric jet of the original initial slice. The reduction's equations propagate $q_i-\\bar\\nabla_i g=0$ by the homogeneous background-commutator ODE, so $V$ has the metric derivative identities and regularity of [F1]. Its zero initial Maxwell divergence residuals from step 4.1 propagate by the normal-form transport argument of [F1]; the coordinate time foliation is smooth and its normal is $C^1$ at this regularity. Thus both full Maxwell equations hold, and their stress is conserved. The harmonic residual identity from [F1] gives the homogeneous wave equation for $H$. Initially $H=0$ by step 4.1, so its tangential derivatives vanish. In any orthonormal frame with future unit time vector $n$, the Einstein residual is $E_{ab}=\\tfrac12(\\nabla_aH_b+\\nabla_bH_a-g_{ab}\\nabla_cH^c)$, hence $E(n,n)=\\tfrac12\\nabla_nH_n$ and $E(n,\\mathsf e_i)=\\tfrac12\\nabla_nH_i$ there. The endpoint Einstein constraints from step 4.1 give $\\nabla_nH=0$. The same local wave-energy uniqueness argument of [F1], valid for this positive symmetric wave block with bounded coefficient derivatives, yields $H=0$ near the endpoint slice. Therefore $V$ is a full solution.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "L1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "step": "6.1",
          "claim": "On the overlap before $T$, compare $V$ and the old solution $U$ using the difference-energy estimate [F2] on $[t,t']$ with $t<t'<T$, in the reverse time direction. The compact state sets and $H^s$ norms of both solutions have common bounds, so its constant is uniform as $t'\\uparrow T$. Their strong $H^{s-1}$ traces both tend to $U_T$, giving $\\|U(t)-V(t)\\|_{H^{s-1}}\\le C e^{C(t'-t)}\\|U(t')-V(t')\\|_{H^{s-1}}\\to0$. Thus the solutions agree on the overlap, and $V$ extends the original full development through $T$. The same estimate identifies any two continuations. Time reversal gives the left-endpoint case. On each common interval the same [F2] estimate gives the stated $H^{s-1}$ continuous dependence, with constants depending on the coefficient time slab, compact state set, norm bound and $s$.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 5.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
