    "thm-emg-smooth-local-geometric-uniqueness": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "def-emg-electrovacuum-cauchy-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For the SI interpretation, $c$ has units $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, $G_N$ has units $\\mathrm{m^3\\,kg^{-1}\\,s^{-2}}$, $\\mu_0$ has units $\\mathrm{N\\,A^{-2}}$, $\\kappa$ has units $\\mathrm{s^2\\,kg^{-1}\\,m^{-1}}$, and $\\Lambda$ has units $\\mathrm{m^{-2}}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "thm-emg-gauss-codazzi-maxwell-constraints",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$$D^jK_{ji}-D_i\\operatorname{tr}_hK=-\\frac{\\kappa}{\\mu_0}(e\\times_hb)_i.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L3",
          "source": "lem-emg-maxwell-stress-conservation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In the source-free case $J=0$, $\\nabla^aT^{\\rm EM}_{ab}=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L4",
          "source": "lem-emg-fixed-smooth-background-harmonic-data",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then each entry of this first metric jet has the regularity required by the first-order state class: the metric values are $H^{s+1}$ and the prescribed first derivatives are $H^s$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-emg-fieldstrength-positive-symmetric-reduction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In the adapted coordinates its time matrix is $\\alpha^{-1}I_6$, and every coordinate spatial matrix is symmetric.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-gr-reduced-einstein-principal-part",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The resulting first-order system for $u$ is symmetric hyperbolic on every\nregion where $g^{00}<0$ and the spatial block $(g^{ij})$ is positive definite:\nthe coefficient of $\\bar\\nabla_0u$ is the block diagonal\n$\\operatorname{diag}(I,-g^{00}I,g^{ij}I)$ (on the three slots\n$g,p,q$), which is positive definite there, and the coefficients of the spatial\nderivatives are symmetric.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-emg-coupled-reduced-einstein-evolution",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume an open admissible state set $O$ on which $g$ is Lorentzian, $g^{00}<0$, the inverse spatial block $(g^{ij})$ is positive definite, the lapse is positive, and the chosen state variables and coefficients are smooth.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-emg-local-high-sobolev-electrovacuum",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then there are $T>0$ and a solution of the full source-free Einstein–Maxwell system on $(-T,T)\\times\\mathbb T^3$ with\n$$g\\in L^\\infty H^{s+1}\\cap C^0H^s,\\qquad \\partial_tg\\in L^\\infty H^s\\cap C^0H^{s-1},\\qquad F\\in L^\\infty H^s\\cap C^0H^{s-1}.$$\nThe initial two-form is reconstructed by $F(n,\\mathsf e_i)=-e_i$ and $F(\\mathsf e_i,\\mathsf e_j)=\\epsilon_{ijk}b_k$; no independent Maxwell normal derivative is prescribed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-emg-maxwell-gauss-propagation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a smooth oriented four-manifold with a time-oriented $C^1$ Lorentzian metric $g$ of signature $(-,+,+,+)$. Let a neighborhood of the initial slice $\\Sigma_0$ have a $C^1$ spacelike foliation with a $C^1$ future unit normal field $n$, transverse to $\\Sigma_0$. Here the normal's $C^1$ regularity is an explicit hypothesis; a merely $C^1$ defining time function alone need not provide it. Smooth coordinate time levels with a $C^1$ metric satisfy this hypothesis. Let $F$ be a $C^1$ real two-form on this neighborhood. Define its electric and magnetic spatial vectors by $e_i=F(\\mathsf e_i,n)$ and $b_i=\\tfrac12\\epsilon_i{}^{jk}F(\\mathsf e_j,\\mathsf e_k)$ in a positively oriented orthonormal spatial frame. Suppose the six evolution equations are\n$$\\iota_n dF=0,\\qquad \\iota_n d(\\star_gF)=0,$$\nand suppose the initial Gauss constraints are $D_ie^i=0$ and $D_ib^i=0$ on $\\Sigma_0$, where $D$ is the Levi-Civita derivative of its induced $C^1$ Riemannian metric $h$. These six equations are the normal-slot component equations of the field-strength reduction [[lem-emg-fieldstrength-positive-symmetric-reduction]]; their displayed first-derivative formulation is used here directly, without assuming that supplier's smooth-field hypotheses. Then $dF=0$ and $d(\\star_gF)=0$ on a neighborhood of each point of $\\Sigma_0$. No Einstein equation, stress conservation law, smooth potential, or global exactness of $F$ is needed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-emg-einstein-gauge-propagation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(g,F)$ be a reduced Einstein–Maxwell solution with $g\\in C^3$ and $F\\in C^2$ on a neighborhood of a smooth spacelike initial hypersurface $\\Sigma_0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-gr-local-geometric-development",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The reduction propagates its auxiliary compatibility constraints, so the reconstructed fields solve $\\mathcal E(g,\\Psi)=0$ on the reduced solution, before imposing $H=0$ or the unreduced Einstein equations.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-emg-smooth-geometric-causal-research-suppliers",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a time-oriented smooth globally hyperbolic Lorentzian four-manifold with the causal-curve and global-hyperbolicity conventions of [[def-gr-causal-conditions-and-global-hyperbolicity]] and [[def-gr-causal-curves-and-reachability]].",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "On the abstract initial bundle $\\mathbb RN\\oplus T\\Sigma$, the displayed formulas assign the three normal-spatial components and the three spatial-spatial components of an antisymmetric two-form. These are all six independent components of a two-form in four dimensions, so they determine a unique complete initial $F$ without choosing a potential or assuming global exactness. They are smooth because $h,e,b$ are smooth and the reconstruction is tensorial under oriented orthonormal-frame changes.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L1",
            "L2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "In each relatively compact initial coordinate ball choose an orthogonal chart and the initial harmonic jet [L4]. The metric block has time matrix $\\operatorname{diag}(I,-g^{00}I,g^{ij}I)$ by [F1]'s reduced Einstein principal-part supplier, and the Maxwell block has time matrix $\\alpha^{-1}I_6$ by [F1]. On the open state neighborhood where $g^{00}<0$, $(g^{ij})>0$ and $\\alpha>0$, their block sum is positive definite; every spatial matrix is symmetric. Since $T(g,F)$ is algebraic in the state, it contributes only to lower-order terms and creates no off-diagonal derivative blocks. The prescribed local high-Sobolev construction [F2] applies after the compact chart localization in its proof and yields a reduced solution. Its derivative relations propagate; [F3] restores both Maxwell equations and the full Einstein equation on a smaller local domain. Every smooth physical solution in the same harmonic chart extracts to this same coefficient system because the component equations are precisely the harmonic-reduced Einstein equation and field-strength Maxwell evolution. This verifies the local symmetric-hyperbolic, recovery and compatibility hypotheses of [F4].",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L4",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "The equations $dF=0$ and $d\\star_gF=0$ are covariant: pullback commutes with exterior differentiation, and for an orientation-preserving diffeomorphism $\\phi$, $\\phi^*(\\star_gF)=\\star_{\\phi^*g}(\\phi^*F)$. The algebraic stress $T(g,F)$ is likewise natural under pullback. Thus the matter field, equations, stress and initial reconstruction define one fixed covariant model of the kind required by [F4]; no coordinate-dependent potential gauge is introduced.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "L1",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "step": "2.2",
          "claim": "By [L3], the reconstructed source-free Maxwell field has $\\nabla^aT_{ab}=0$ whenever its Maxwell equations hold, independently of the unreduced Einstein equation. The constraints in the Statement are exactly [L2]'s four normal-projection constraints with the same sign, orientation, $\\kappa,\\mu_0,\\Lambda$ and $K$ conventions. This verifies the conservation and Einstein-constraint hypotheses of [F4].",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "L2",
            "L3",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "We have now checked every specific input of [F4]: a fixed covariant model by step 2.1, complete matter data and smooth initialization by step 1.1 and [L4], a smooth symmetric-hyperbolic reduction and reconstruction by step 1.2, on-shell stress conservation by step 2.2, and the model's Einstein constraints by [L2]. The choice assumptions are those in the Statement. Applying [F4] gives the smooth local development, variable-width collar, intrinsic globally hyperbolic Cauchy neighborhood and data-fixing isometry matching $F$. The causal qualifications in [F5] confirm that no compactness or uniform collar width is used.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "step 1.2",
            "L2",
            "L3",
            "L4",
            "F4",
            "F5"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
