    "lem-emg-smooth-geometric-causal-research-suppliers": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-gr-convex-normal-neighborhoods",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(b) Unique contained joining segment.** For all $q,q'\\in C$ there is\nexactly one geodesic segment joining $q$ to $q'$ whose image is contained in\n$C$, namely\n$$x_{q,q'}:[0,1]\\to C,\\qquad x_{q,q'}(t)=\\exp_q\\bigl(t\\,v(q,q')\\bigr),$$\nwhere $v(q,q')$ is the two-point inverse velocity of\n[[lem-gr-joint-exponential-diffeomorphism]] clause (c).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-gr-normal-neighborhood-causal-cone",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $C\\subseteq M$ be a strictly\nconvex normal set ([[def-gr-normal-neighborhoods-and-position-vectors]]\nclause (b)), let $p\\in C$, and write\n$$W_p:=\\exp_p^{-1}(C)\\subseteq T_pM,\\qquad v(q):=\\exp_p^{-1}(q),\\qquad P(p,q):=d(\\exp_p)_{v(q)}(v(q)),\\qquad D_p^2(q):=g_p(v(q),v(q)).$$\nDefine\n$$J_C^+(p):=\\{p\\}\\cup\\{q\\in C:\\text{there is a future causal curve } \\sigma:[0,1]\\to C\\text{ with }\\sigma(0)=p,\\ \\sigma(1)=q\\},$$\nso the reflexive endpoint $p$ is included even though a constant curve is\nnot in the curve class.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-gr-auxiliary-complete-riemannian-metric",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(c)** For each connected component $M_0$ of $M$, the metric space\n$(M_0,d_{h'})$ is complete, where $d_{h'}$ is the Riemannian distance of\n$h'|_{M_0}$ ([[def-riemannian-distance-on-a-connected-manifold]],\n[[thm-riemannian-distance-is-a-metric]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-gr-globally-hyperbolic-causal-structure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a\ntime-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold that is globally hyperbolic in\nthe sense of [[def-gr-causal-conditions-and-global-hyperbolicity]] clause\n(c): strongly causal and with compact causal diamonds\n$J^+(p)\\cap J^-(q)$ for all $p,q\\in M$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-gr-limit-curve-two-endpoints",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Endpoint convergence puts the joining curves in the component of $x$ for all sufficiently large $k$, since components of a manifold are open.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-gr-time-separation-maximizers",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a\nglobally hyperbolic time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold\n([[def-gr-causal-conditions-and-global-hyperbolicity]] clause (c)), let\n$L_g$ be the Lebesgue Lorentzian length functional on locally absolutely continuous future causal curves\n([[lem-gr-normal-neighborhood-length-monotonicity]], with nonzero future causal velocity almost everywhere), and for $q\\in M$ define the\n**time separation**\n$$\\tau_q:M\\to[0,\\infty),\\qquad \\tau_q(r):=\\sup\\{L_g(\\alpha):\\ \\alpha \\text{ is a future causal curve from } r \\text{ to } q\\},$$\nthe supremum over the empty set being $0$; thus $\\tau_q(r)>0$ exactly for\n$r\\in I^-(q)$ and $\\tau_q(r)=0$ for $r\\notin J^-(q)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-gr-local-cauchy-neighborhood",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(d) Generic spacelike and noncompact scope.** For every smooth embedded spacelike hypersurface $\\Sigma\\subseteq M$ without boundary there exist an open neighborhood $U_\\Sigma$ of $\\Sigma$, a smooth positive function $\\delta:\\Sigma\\to(0,\\infty)$, and a diffeomorphism\n$$F:\\Omega\\to U_\\Sigma,\\qquad\\Omega=\\{(s,x)\\in\\mathbb R\\times\\Sigma:|s|<\\delta(x)\\},\\qquad F(0,x)=x,$$\nsuch that $s$ is temporal and $F_*\\partial_s$ is future timelike.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-gr-cauchy-surface-implies-globally-hyperbolic",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(c)** for all $p,q\\in M$ the causal diamond\n$J^+(p)\\cap J^-(q)$ is compact; consequently $(M,g,N)$ is globally\nhyperbolic in the sense of clause (c) of that definition.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-gr-smooth-cauchy-temporal-splitting",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(b)** Every level set $S_c:=T^{-1}(c)$, $c\\in\\mathbb R$, is a Cauchy\nhypersurface ([[def-gr-spacelike-hypersurfaces-and-cauchy-sets]] clauses (a)\nand (c)): it is a spacelike embedded hypersurface and every inextendible\ncausal curve meets it in exactly one parameter value.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Apply [F1] at each point of $M$, with an arbitrary prescribed open neighborhood to obtain the local normal neighborhood. The first supplier gives the unique geodesic segment and strict convexity; the second applies to that same set and gives exactly the exponential-cone, null-rigidity and length conclusions in clause 1.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Apply [F2]. The time orientation gives a smooth timelike field needed for its auxiliary Riemannian construction, and the assumed global hyperbolicity supplies strong causality and compact diamonds. The resulting complete auxiliary metric is precisely the metric required by the causal-structure lemma; its conclusions give clause 2.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "step": "1.3",
          "claim": "The assumed manifold is globally hyperbolic, so [F4] applies to every fixed endpoint $q$. Its definition of $\\tau_q$ as a supremum and its compact-diamond argument give finiteness and attainment; the maximizing-curve argument gives a causal geodesic; its local normal-neighborhood formula gives continuity on timelike-related points, while the endpoint-sequence argument gives upper semicontinuity on compact sets. The $r=q$ case has length zero by definition and is not silently treated as a nondegenerate curve. This proves clause 4 with exactly its endpoint qualifications.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "The hypotheses of [F3] are exactly Countable Choice, Dependent Choice, the smooth time-oriented Lorentzian manifold, and the complete auxiliary metric from step 2. Its bounded-length case supplies a limiting causal curve with both endpoint limits; its unbounded case supplies the stated inextendible future and past curves and sequential causal relation. Thus clause 3 is the corresponding supplier conclusion, including the coincident-endpoint and imprisonment cases only when their stated hypotheses hold.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.2",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "For any smooth embedded spacelike hypersurface, apply [F5]'s local-neighborhood clause. Its width is a positive function on the hypersurface, and its domain of dependence is intrinsic to that neighborhood; the Cauchy and global-hyperbolicity conclusions are therefore precisely those in clause 5, without compactness or uniform-width assumptions. For any globally hyperbolic spacetime, the temporal-splitting supplier applies under the same choice assumptions and gives its product decomposition.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F5"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement clause2 allows compact sets with empty causal hull: Jplus(empty) intersect Jminus(K)=empty, a compact set. Proof step 1.1 is pointwise, so an empty spacetime adds no falsely selected normal neighborhood."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
