    "thm-emg-sourcefree-smooth-maximal-development": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "def-emg-electrovacuum-cauchy-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "No global electromagnetic potential is part of this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "thm-emg-gauss-codazzi-maxwell-constraints",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then the induced tuple $(\\Sigma,h,K,e,b)$ satisfies all four constraints in [[def-emg-constrained-electrovacuum-data]], namely the two Maxwell divergence constraints and",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L3",
          "source": "lem-emg-maxwell-stress-conservation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In the source-free case $J=0$, $\\nabla^aT^{\\rm EM}_{ab}=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-emg-smooth-local-geometric-uniqueness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Any two developments of this same model and complete data are related near the entire initial surface by a data-fixing, time-orientation-preserving isometry carrying $F$ to $F$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-emg-smooth-geometric-causal-research-suppliers",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a time-oriented smooth globally hyperbolic Lorentzian four-manifold with the causal-curve and global-hyperbolicity conventions of [[def-gr-causal-conditions-and-global-hyperbolicity]] and [[def-gr-causal-curves-and-reachability]].",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-gr-development-and-data-fixing-isometry",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(d) Maximality.** A development $(M,g,N,j,\\psi)$ of the given data is\n**maximal** when for every development $(M',g',N',j',\\psi')$ of the same data\nthere exists a development embedding $\\varphi:M'\\to M$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-gr-smooth-maximal-globally-hyperbolic-development",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The supplied local existence and uniqueness obligations must hold for that same model and for the complete data at every spacelike restart used in the proof.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "The initial bundle formulas specify the three normal-spatial and three spatial-spatial components of $F$ and hence all independent matter data, as in [F1]'s complete-data reconstruction. The four displayed constraints are exactly [L2]'s hypotheses with the same stress normalization, orientation and sign convention. Thus the data are complete constrained data for the one fixed EMG model; no assumption that $F=dA$ is introduced.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L1",
            "L2",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "By [F1], every smooth constrained initial data set for this fixed model has a smooth local development and data-fixing geometric uniqueness. The same statement applies to every smooth spacelike restart: the full Einstein and Maxwell equations hold there; their normal projections give [L2]'s constraints; the normal electric and spatial magnetic components determine the complete restarted two-form; and [L3] supplies stress conservation after Maxwell propagation. Therefore the local existence and uniqueness obligations are available at every restart used in [F4].",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "L2",
            "L3",
            "F1",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "In particular, [F1] provides at least one local development. Its local Cauchy-neighborhood clause, also covered by [F2], restricts it to an open globally hyperbolic neighborhood with $\\Sigma$ Cauchy, so the nonempty-development hypothesis of [F4] holds. The model and complete data remain fixed under all comparisons, and the Countable Choice and Dependent Choice assumptions match [F4].",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "Apply [F4] to this fixed model, data, local development and restart theorem. Its universality clause gives a data-fixing development embedding of every smooth globally hyperbolic development into one maximal development; its uniqueness clause gives exactly one data-fixing isometry between any two maximal developments. By [F3], this is precisely maximality in the stated smooth globally hyperbolic class. The definition imposes no condition on affine-parameter endpoints of geodesics, so this conclusion contains no geodesic-completeness assertion.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
